Stop, with a message, instead of corrupting memory when an allocation
fails or a size does not fit in an int (docs/M1-REPORT.md, section 7.4).

- Without TCL_MEM_DEBUG, ckalloc was malloc, and Tcl 6 never checks the
  result. It is now TclCkalloc, which panics ("tcl16: out of memory")
  if malloc returns NULL.
- Tcl's string and list sizes are ints, 16 bits on DOS, and its buffers
  grow by doubling, which overflows from 16384 bytes (doubling a
  20480-byte string, or "append s $s" on a 30000-byte one, overran the
  heap and hung DOSBox-X). The growth of variable values
  (Tcl_SetVar2), the result (Tcl_AppendResult, SetupAppendBuffer), the
  format buffer, the parse buffer (TclExpandParseValue) and the sizes
  of Tcl_ScanElement, Tcl_Merge and Tcl_Concat are worked out in long
  by the new TclGrowSize, which keeps them within INT_MAX and panics
  ("tcl16: string or list too long") when even the size needed does
  not fit; and the comparisons that decide whether those buffers must
  grow (Tcl_SetVar2, format) are made in long too, since 30000 + 30000
  is negative in an int and the buffer was then overrun. These are the
  sites found in M1, not an audit of every int-sized length.
- format's field width and precision were read with atoi, so that
  "%40000s" became "%-25536s" and overran the result: they are read
  in long and are an error past INT_MAX.
- panic flushes stdout first, so that output printed before it is
  kept.

diff -ru a/panic.c b/panic.c
--- a/panic.c
+++ b/panic.c
@@ -20,6 +20,7 @@
 #include <stdio.h>
 #include <stdlib.h>
 #include <stdarg.h>
+#include <limits.h>
 
 /*
  *----------------------------------------------------------------------
@@ -43,9 +44,72 @@
 {
     va_list argList;
 
+    (void) fflush(stdout);	/* tcl16: keep what was printed before. */
     va_start(argList, format);
     (void) vfprintf(stderr, format, argList);
     va_end(argList);
     (void) fflush(stderr);
     abort();
 }
+
+/*
+ *----------------------------------------------------------------------
+ *
+ * TclCkalloc --
+ *
+ *	tcl16: ckalloc without TCL_MEM_DEBUG. Tcl 6 never checks what
+ *	malloc returns, so running out of memory would write through a
+ *	null pointer; this stops the interpreter with a message instead.
+ *
+ * Results:
+ *	The new block.
+ *
+ * Side effects:
+ *	Panics if there is no memory.
+ *
+ *----------------------------------------------------------------------
+ */
+
+char *
+TclCkalloc(unsigned int size)
+{
+    char *p = (char *) malloc(size);
+
+    if (p == NULL) {
+	panic("tcl16: out of memory (%u bytes wanted)\n", size);
+    }
+    return p;
+}
+
+/*
+ *----------------------------------------------------------------------
+ *
+ * TclGrowSize --
+ *
+ *	tcl16: the size of a growing buffer, worked out in long because
+ *	Tcl's sizes are ints, which have 16 bits on DOS: "want" (usually
+ *	twice the old size) if an int can hold it, else INT_MAX, but at
+ *	least "need".
+ *
+ * Results:
+ *	The size, an int.
+ *
+ * Side effects:
+ *	Panics if even "need" is more than an int can hold: one string or
+ *	list of more than INT_MAX bytes.
+ *
+ *----------------------------------------------------------------------
+ */
+
+int
+TclGrowSize(long need, long want)
+{
+    if ((need < 0) || (need > INT_MAX)) {
+	panic("tcl16: string or list too long (%ld bytes; the most is %d)\n",
+		need, INT_MAX);
+    }
+    if ((want < need) || (want > INT_MAX)) {
+	want = (want < need) ? need : INT_MAX;
+    }
+    return (int) want;
+}
